<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baku–Tbilisi–Ceyhan pipeline</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as BTC pipeline</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>–Ceyhan pipeline carries crude oil about 1,768 km from the Caspian oilfields near Baku in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> to the Turkish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> port of Ceyhan. Built by a BP-led consortium and opened in 2005, it was designed to bring Caspian oil to Western markets while bypassing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baku-tbilisi-ceyhan-pipeline.jpg" alt="Baku–Tbilisi–Ceyhan pipeline" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baku%E2%80%93Tbilisi%E2%80%93Ceyhan_pipeline_(BTC_Pipeline).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> lies above one of the largest collections of oil and gas fields, but being landlocked it makes the export of oil to Western markets difficult. In Soviet times every transport route from the region ran through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. After the Soviet collapse, producers sought new routes. Russia first insisted that a new pipeline cross its territory and then declined to take part.</p><p>In the spring of 1992 the Turkish prime minister Süleyman Demirel proposed a route through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and Turkey signed the first document on the project on 9 March 1993. A route through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> was politically impossible because of the unresolved war over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a>, which left the longer and costlier route through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. The Ankara Declaration of 29 October 1998 gave the project momentum, and Azerbaijan, Georgia and Turkey signed an intergovernmental agreement on 18 November 1999 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The pipeline is 1,768 km long and runs from the Sangachal Terminal near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> to the Ceyhan Marine Terminal on the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Of its length, 443 km lie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, 249 km in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and 1,076 km in Turkey. It crosses mountain ranges at up to 2,830 m and passes under or over 3,000 roads, railways and utility lines and 1,500 watercourses. It is buried at least a metre deep and measures 1,070 mm in diameter for most of its length, narrowing to 865 mm near Ceyhan. Eight pump stations push the oil at about two metres a second, and at normal capacity it carries one million barrels a day.</p><p>It takes oil chiefly from the Azeri-Chirag-Guneshli field in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. Kazakh oil has also reached it by tanker across the Caspian. The line cost US$3.9 billion, of which 70 per cent came from third parties including the International Finance Corporation and the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>BTC Co. was established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 1 August 2002, with BP managing the consortium. Construction began in April 2003. The pipeline was inaugurated at the Sangachal Terminal on 25 May 2005, and the first oil reached Ceyhan on 28 May 2006. Archaeological surveys sponsored by BP began in 2000, and construction uncovered cultural artefacts, leading to coordinated research at sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><p>The pipeline drew criticism for its environmental risks, notably its route through the watershed of the Borjomi-Kharagauli National Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and for the bypassing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Its supporters in Georgia saw it as a guarantee of the country's independence and security. It removed about 350 tanker cargoes a year from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a> and Dardanelles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baku–Tbilisi–Ceyhan pipeline” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Baku–Tbilisi–Ceyhan pipeline</b>, 1,774 km through Azerbaijan, Georgia and Turkey, opened in May 2006 and can carry 50 million tons of crude a year; the South Caucasus Pipeline followed at the end of 2006, and the Trans-Anatolian and Trans Adriatic pipelines deliver Shah Deniz gas to Turkey and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Georgia is a transit corridor between Europe and Asia bypassing Russia, through Batumi and Poti, the <b>Baku–Tbilisi–Ceyhan pipeline</b> and the Baku–Tbilisi–Kars railway opened in 2017.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
